WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:05.439
A payment authorization takes 200 ms.

2
00:00:05.439 --> 00:00:12.240
It parses the request, calls the fraud service, the card issuer and the ledger, then replies.

3
00:00:12.467 --> 00:00:15.394
Only 5 ms of that runs on a CPU.

4
00:00:15.394 --> 00:00:19.328
For the other 195, it waits on the network.

5
00:00:20.067 --> 00:00:26.601
The server gives this request one thread, and the request keeps that thread for all 200 ms.

6
00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:29.760
While the request waits, its thread waits with it.

7
00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:33.491
A blocked thread does no work, but no other request can use it.

8
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.637
A server keeps a pool of 200 such threads.

9
00:00:37.637 --> 00:00:39.270
Each square is one thread.

10
00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:41.279
It fills up as its request runs.

11
00:00:41.279 --> 00:00:45.800
When it is full, the request is done, and the thread takes the next one.

12
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:52.876
At 800 requests a second, about 160 threads are busy at any moment, and 40 sit idle.

13
00:00:53.067 --> 00:00:56.728
Now traffic rises to 1,500 a second.

14
00:00:56.728 --> 00:00:58.051
What happens?

15
00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:02.248
The idle threads fill up at once.

16
00:01:02.248 --> 00:01:04.545
Then new requests have nowhere to go.

17
00:01:04.545 --> 00:01:06.407
They wait in the pool's queue.

18
00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:12.586
Each thread finishes one request every 200 ms, so five a second.

19
00:01:12.586 --> 00:01:16.800
200 threads finish 1,000 a second, and not one more.

20
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:19.183
The other 500 a second pile up.

21
00:01:19.183 --> 00:01:27.211
After ten seconds, about 5,000 are waiting, and a new request waits five seconds before a thread even picks it up.

22
00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:28.850
Freeze the picture.

23
00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:32.286
Of the 200 threads, about 5 are running code.

24
00:01:32.286 --> 00:01:36.103
The other 195 are blocked, waiting on the network.

25
00:01:36.267 --> 00:01:39.857
You run out of threads long before you run out of CPU.

26
00:01:40.733 --> 00:01:45.812
Now the card issuer has a bad day, and its calls take 1.2 seconds.

27
00:01:45.812 --> 00:01:49.429
Each request holds its thread for 1.28 seconds.

28
00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:53.398
The same 200 threads now finish only 156 a second.

29
00:01:53.398 --> 00:01:55.298
Your code did not change.

30
00:01:55.298 --> 00:01:59.476
A slow service downstream took your throughput with it.

31
00:02:00.467 --> 00:02:03.154
Why not raise the pool to 2,000 threads?

32
00:02:03.154 --> 00:02:05.438
It does lift the cap, for a while.

33
00:02:05.438 --> 00:02:10.343
But each of these is a platform thread: one Java thread is one OS thread.

34
00:02:10.533 --> 00:02:15.570
Each one reserves a stack up front: 1 MB by default on 64-bit Linux.

35
00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:20.680
The kernel adds guard pages and its own bookkeeping for every thread.

36
00:02:20.867 --> 00:02:22.813
Starting one is a system call.

37
00:02:22.813 --> 00:02:29.883
And when thousands are runnable, switching between them costs the kernel time, and costs you your CPU caches.

38
00:02:30.867 --> 00:02:36.804
So a JVM is comfortable with hundreds of platform threads, and survives a few thousand.

39
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:41.482
At tens of thousands it fights OS limits, memory and the scheduler.

40
00:02:41.482 --> 00:02:43.221
A million is out of reach.

41
00:02:44.133 --> 00:02:46.399
This is the thread-per-request wall.

42
00:02:46.399 --> 00:02:52.253
Each request holds a thread while it waits, and platform threads are too costly to have many.

43
00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:55.172
Little's law puts a number on it.

44
00:02:55.172 --> 00:02:59.875
Requests in flight equal arrival rate times time inside.

45
00:02:59.875 --> 00:03:03.738
1,500 a second for 0.2 seconds is 300 threads.

46
00:03:03.738 --> 00:03:08.021
With 200, the most you can serve is 1,000 a second.

47
00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:15.706
In code, it is the plain blocking style: a fixed pool, and each task keeps its thread from start to end.

48
00:03:15.706 --> 00:03:17.432
Kotlin uses the same pool.

49
00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:19.684
There are two ways out.

50
00:03:19.684 --> 00:03:25.105
Make each request wait less, which you often can't, because the issuer is not yours.

51
00:03:25.105 --> 00:03:29.300
Or hold a waiting request in something cheaper than an OS thread.

52
00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:31.688
Virtual threads take that second way.

53
00:03:32.533 --> 00:03:33.174
The rule.

54
00:03:33.174 --> 00:03:40.077
A blocked request still holds its thread, so threads divided by time per request is your ceiling.

55
00:03:40.267 --> 00:03:41.287
Check yourself.

56
00:03:41.287 --> 00:03:44.687
200 threads, and each request now takes 2 seconds.

57
00:03:44.687 --> 00:03:47.476
How many can the service finish a second?

58
00:03:50.133 --> 00:03:50.540
100.

59
00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:53.894
200 threads divided by 2 seconds.

60
00:03:53.894 --> 00:03:56.639
The idle CPU does not help.
